diff --git a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
index bdc77b1..ef2368e 100644
--- a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
+++ b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
@@ -26,7 +26,7 @@ The headline scenario; see `diagrams/write-path-sequence.mermaid`. Detailed step
 See `diagrams/read-path-sequence.mermaid`.
 
 1. **Resolve the home zone (L2).** Resolve the path/key against the global namespace → file identity, **home zone**, and an ACL check (cached after first use). Consistency-sensitive reads route to the home zone (section 6.6); stale-tolerant reads may target a nearer replica.
-2. **Fetch the chunk map (L4).** From the home zone's metadata store — or a version-fenced replica — get the chunk list, fragment locations, and EC scheme (cached after first read). In steady state both metadata hops are cached, so a hot read goes straight to the data path.
+2. **Fetch the chunk map (L4).** From the home zone's metadata store — or a version-fenced replica — get the chunk list, fragment locations, and EC scheme (cached after first read). In steady state both metadata hops are cached, so a hot read goes straight to the data path. One metadata value has a size ceiling, so a large object's map is **segmented**: the root carries the group identity and a segment table, and resolving it is the root plus **one bounded range read** of that group's own records, ordered by **parsed index** — never a global scan, and a table naming more segments than the reader's own ceiling allows is refused **unread**, as is a segment record whose value is past the ceiling any backend would have stored — how much work a record can demand of a reader is the reader's number, not the record's. Resolution is **single-sourced**: one call turns a committed root into that ordered list, so how an object's chunks are learned is written once, not once per consumer. The read paths — whole-object, streaming and ranged — resolve through that call rather than reading the root's own field; a consumer that has not yet adopted it refuses a segmented map outright rather than answering from the root alone — an under-approximation of which bytes an object owns is what lets one process reclaim what another still needs. A generation retired mid-resolve (the root moved on, or is gone) drops the stale resolution and **restarts against the live root** — never a torn or empty answer; an anomaly on a generation the root still names — an absent, undecodable, or mismatched segment — **fails closed** with a typed error, scoped to that one object.
 3. **Read fragments directly from D servers.** Holding the EC logic, the client needs only *k* of *n* fragments and reconstructs from whichever *k* arrive first — turning erasure coding into a tail-latency *advantage*: a slow or dead disk does not slow the read.
 4. **Verify checksums** on every fragment, catching bit rot at read time; re-read elsewhere on failure.
 
